Your app hits **four** .NET 11 behavioral breaking changes. Here's what breaks and how to fix each:

---

### 1. GZipStream empty payload now writes headers/footers
Your zero-byte check for "no data" will **false-negative** — empty payloads now produce valid GZip headers/footers (length > 0).

**Fix:** Check the uncompressed byte count instead of output stream length:
```csharp
// Before (.NET 10): ms.Length == 0 meant "no data"
// After (.NET 11): ms.Length > 0 even with no data written

// Fix: track whether you actually wrote any data
bool wroteData = false;
using (var gz = new GZipStream(ms, CompressionMode.Compress, leaveOpen: true))
{
    if (hasData) { gz.Write(data); wroteData = true; }
}
if (!wroteData) { /* handle "no data" */ }
```

---

### 2. TAR header checksum validation now enforced
Your legacy hand-crafted TAR files with invalid checksums will now **throw exceptions** on read.

**Fix:** Add error handling around `TarReader`:
```csharp
try
{
    using var reader = new TarReader(stream);
    var entry = reader.GetNextEntry();
}
catch (InvalidDataException ex)
{
    // Handle legacy TAR files with bad checksums
    logger.LogWarning("Invalid TAR checksum: {Message}", ex.Message);
}
```
Long-term: re-archive legacy files with valid checksums.

---

### 3. ZipArchive.CreateAsync now eagerly loads entries
You relied on lazy loading, but `CreateAsync` now **eagerly loads all entries into memory**. For large ZIP files, this increases memory pressure.

**Fix:** Be aware of the higher memory footprint. For very large archives, consider processing entries in smaller batches or streaming approaches.

---

### 4. MemoryStream maximum capacity changed
The maximum capacity and exception behavior have changed. Your very large allocations near the old max may now **throw different exceptions or behave differently**.

**Fix:** Review any code catching specific exception types (e.g., `OutOfMemoryException` vs `IOException`) around large `MemoryStream` allocations, and test with your actual payload sizes.

---

### TFM update
Don't forget to update the target framework:
```xml
<!-- Before -->
<TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
<!-- After -->
<TargetFramework>net11.0</TargetFramework>
```

The GZipStream and TAR checksum changes are the most likely to cause **silent bugs** or **runtime failures** respectively. I'd prioritize fixing those first. Want me to look at your actual source code and apply these fixes?
